# Canonical SMC TLM sideband extension

Every SMC IP and peripheral — existing or newly modeled — must carry AXI4
sideband metadata on its TLM-2.0 transactions using the **single shared**
type defined in `smc/common/include/smc_axi_extension.h`:

- `smc::smc_axi_extension` — the `tlm_extension` carrying `source_id`,
  `axi_id`, `prot`, `is_locked`/`is_fetch`/`is_secure`/`is_user`, `axi_user`,
  plus the `set_priv`/`set_secure`/`set_fetch`/`set_locked` coherence setters.
- `smc::source_id_t` — the `uint16_t` enum (`SMC_ID=0x10` … `SYS_ID=0x60`).

This is the **only** definition of those types in the tree. Do not
re-derive, re-declare, or duplicate either of them in a peripheral tree.

## Why this is mandatory

A platform binary links multiple SMC modules together (cpu_cluster +
smc_fabric + several peripherals). If two TUs see different definitions of
`smc::smc_axi_extension` under the same name, `trans.get_extension<smc_axi_extension>()`
reinterprets one byte layout through another — `source_id` widens/narrows,
`cacheable`/`non_secure` read from where `is_locked`/`is_fetch` were written,
etc. That is an ODR violation and a silent data-corruption bug at the fabric
boundary. One canonical header makes the contract hold by construction.

## How to use it

Add the shared directory to the target's include path and include the header:

```cmake
# In the peripheral's CMakeLists.txt — add to target_include_directories:
target_include_directories(smc_<ip> PUBLIC
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../../common/include   # smc-scoped (smc_axi_extension.h)
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../../../common/include # repo common (sim_log.h, reg_access.h)
)
```

```cpp
#include "smc_axi_extension.h"   // resolves to smc/common/include/smc_axi_extension.h

// Attach to an outgoing transaction:
auto* ext = new smc::smc_axi_extension();
ext->source_id = smc::SMC_ID;
ext->set_priv(priv != 0);
gp.set_extension(ext);

// Inspect on an incoming transaction:
if (auto* ext = gp.get_extension<smc::smc_axi_extension>()) {
    filter_allow(addr, ext->source_id, (ext->prot >> 1) & 1u, gp.is_write());
}
```

## Do not

- ❌ Define `smc::smc_axi_extension` or `smc::source_id_t` in a peripheral
  header (no per-peripheral `smc_tlm_extensions.h` shim, no inline copy).
  These patterns were removed from the tree on purpose — see commit
  `15c2690` ("Relocate smc_axi_extension to shared smc/common/include").
- ❌ `#include "smc_axi_extension.h"` from a peripheral's own `include/`
  expecting a local copy — the header lives only in `smc/common/include/`.
- ❌ Invent a parallel sideband type ("my_ip_axi_extension") for an SMC IP
  that talks to the SMC fabric — it will not be ODR-compatible with the
  fabric's `get_extension<smc_axi_extension>()` lookup.
- ❌ Change the field set or `source_id_t` values in one peripheral to
  "fix" a local need — the canonical header is the contract; propose a
  change there so every IP moves together.

## New IP checklist

When adding a new SMC IP/peripheral:

1. `#include "smc_axi_extension.h"` in the IP's main header (do not create
   a local `smc_tlm_extensions.h`).
2. Add `smc/common/include` to the target's `target_include_directories`
   (path is `${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../../common/include` from a
   peripheral, `../common/include` from `smc_fabric`/`cpu_cluster`).
3. Attach `smc_axi_extension` to every outgoing `tlm_generic_payload` and
   read it (via `get_extension<smc_axi_extension>()`) on every inbound
   filter/access-control decision.
4. If you need a new sideband field or a new `source_id_t` value, edit the
   canonical header so all IPs stay aligned — do not fork.

